      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes - the multi-level device is the correct solution.  Ultimately all the I2C accesses should only be done by the parent \"nuvoton,nct38xx\" driver.\n\nEffectively, the existing upstream \"nuvoton,nct38xx-gpio\" driver can get renamed to \"nuvoton,nct38xx\", because it\u0027s handling the I2C transactions.  The interrupt pins should also be a property of this node, instead of having a separate \"gpio-alert\" driver.\n\nI don\u0027t see any need for a separate \"nuvoton,nct38xx-gpio\" driver, so the tree would look like:\n```\nnct38xx@70 {\n\tcompatible \u003d \"nuvoton,nct38xx\";\n\treg \u003d \u003c0x70\u003e;\n\tirq-gpios \u003d \u003c\u0026gpio...\u003e;\n\tioex_port1: gpio@0 {\n\t\tcompatible \u003d \"nuvoton,nct38xx-gpio-port\";\n\t\treg \u003d \u003c0x0\u003e;\n\t};\n\ttcpc_port0: tcpc-port0 {\n\t        compatible \u003d \"nuvoton,nct38xx-tcpc\";\n\t};\n};\n```\n\nThe `gpio-dev` property of \"nuvoton,nct38xx-tcpc\" becomes obsolete - since this is now just the parent device.",
